  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nAnother overnight MCS is ongoing across Nebraska early this morning, \namid weak zonal flow aloft along the northern periphery of the upper \nridge. The trailing outflow boundary from the MCS may briefly shift \nwinds mid morning and increase mid level clouds north of I-70, but \notherwise the residual MCS stays north and east of the area. Sfc \ndewpoints starting out in the low/mid 70s with lows only dropping to \nthe upper 70s should spell another dangerously hot afternoon. The \nExtreme Heat Warning remains in effect as apparent temps reach 110 \nin the afternoon. Near the highway 36 corridor, apparent temps are \nforecast to reach near 105 and with no obvious deterring factor in \nlower temps/dewpoints, decided to issue a Heat Advisory for the far \nnorthern Kansas counties this afternoon. \n\nThe west-east stationary boundary near the I-70 corridor becomes the \nfocus for isolated severe storm development as a sfc low develops \nthis afternoon. Low level convergence along the sfc trough will \nlikely be overcome as forecast soundings mix the boundary layer to \n600 mb. The last several CAMs build upon this confidence in \ndevelopment compared to the less organized setups seen the last \nseveral days. Given the decent DCAPE present and vertical wind \nshear, a few storms may be severe, producing damaging wind gusts and \nmarginally severe hail. If these storms form, they may persist \neastward into the evening, aided by the enhanced LLJ. \n\nPeriodic storm chances continue Friday and Saturday evenings as the \nfrontal boundary lingers across the region. The southwest to \nnortheast orientation of the jet and subsequent MCSs that form over \nthe high plains suggest any residual storms would most likely impact \nnorth central Kansas. Highest chances occur late Saturday evening as \na stronger embedded trough shifts a cold front southward.\nWidespread heat relief arrives behind the front on Sunday, dropping \ntemps into the 80s through early next week. Shortwave perturbations \nembedded in the zonal flow aloft will support low-end precip chances \nnext week, though organized severe weather probabilities are low.",
